1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,685
The name came to us naturally
while we were thinking about the album.

2
00:00:08,685 --> 00:00:14,100
In the end we decided not to make anything up.
We argued for a long time...

3
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:17,574
Well, it was more that
we just had our doubts.

4
00:00:17,574 --> 00:00:20,435
– Yeah, yeah, yeah.
– Well, what are we called? Verification.

5
00:00:20,435 --> 00:00:22,989
The project is called "Verification", yes.

6
00:00:22,989 --> 00:00:27,076
The album has a certain concept
connected with us, with people,

7
00:00:27,076 --> 00:00:29,835
with how we feel about ourselves.
There's this theory

8
00:00:29,835 --> 00:00:33,513
that we all exist in a simulation.
– Oh!
– Right, and the world we live in

9
00:00:33,513 --> 00:00:36,987
is also some kind of simulation.

10
00:00:36,987 --> 00:00:40,666
And we got deep into the question
of how you prove that you're human.

11
00:00:40,666 --> 00:00:45,979
And we came up against the fact
that only a human has certain qualities

12
00:00:45,979 --> 00:00:49,044
that belong to them alone.

13
00:00:57,729 --> 00:01:01,305
Honestly, this is my first
really serious experience...

14
00:01:01,305 --> 00:01:05,903
of bringing such great
musicians into a recording.

15
00:01:19,186 --> 00:01:22,251
It was really great when the guys

16
00:01:22,251 --> 00:01:25,316
got into the groove too, they were so fired up.
Remember, when we were already saying:

17
00:01:25,316 --> 00:01:28,381
"Guys, let's probably keep this take",
and they were like: "No,

18
00:01:28,381 --> 00:01:31,753
let's do one more take."
That was great, you could see they

19
00:01:31,753 --> 00:01:35,023
really cared about the quality.
– Yes, even though it was 3 a.m. by then...

20
00:01:35,023 --> 00:01:40,132
– Yes, they were recording at night
after their daytime orchestra sessions.

21
00:01:49,838 --> 00:01:52,393
Vanya and I have known each other since school.

22
00:01:52,393 --> 00:01:56,480
We spent pretty much our whole childhood together,
studying under the same roof,

23
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:00,567
travelling the world, performing classical music
side by side in a children's choir.

24
00:02:00,567 --> 00:02:06,697
Immersed in classical music, yes. Once we grew up,
our paths went separate ways.

25
00:02:06,697 --> 00:02:10,784
One summer, probably two or three years ago,
I happened to invite Vanya over,

26
00:02:10,784 --> 00:02:14,360
I said: "Come visit, have a look."
Vanya came, took a look at everything,

27
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:18,447
I turned on the piano for him and he started playing.
And that's when I realised that yes, this is probably how

28
00:02:18,447 --> 00:02:21,513
we'll start writing music.
And that day we wrote our first track,

29
00:02:21,513 --> 00:02:25,600
which was actually used...
it probably became the foundation.

30
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:29,176
That track sat on my computer for several years.
Every now and then I'd send the recording to Vanya

31
00:02:29,176 --> 00:02:32,752
and say: "Listen, come on, we need to finish this."
Vanya would say: "I've got concerts."

32
00:02:32,752 --> 00:02:36,328
But he said one thing:
"We'll definitely take this on."

33
00:02:36,328 --> 00:02:40,926
We met again at my home studio
and carried on working.

34
00:03:22,307 --> 00:03:25,883
I'm sure listeners will find this music
fits all kinds of situations in life.

35
00:03:25,883 --> 00:03:30,481
I listened to this music as if it weren't mine,
on my way somewhere.

36
00:03:30,481 --> 00:03:34,568
You place yourself in a space where you're moving,
there's movement and bustle all around you,

37
00:03:34,568 --> 00:03:38,144
you see that movement. And yet you're at
a still point, you're inside this space,

38
00:03:38,144 --> 00:03:41,209
you're not moving. You can relax,
unwind

39
00:03:41,209 --> 00:03:46,318
and watch this fast, fleeting movement
of the world, of molecules, of particles around you.

40
00:03:46,318 --> 00:03:50,916
And at the same time you're in a space
made by human hands for human comfort.

41
00:03:50,916 --> 00:04:07,264
Without even expecting it, you've arrived...
somewhere in another, technical dimension.

42
00:04:09,307 --> 00:04:17,481
About the intonation: yes, you landed on the C: E-D-C-B-C,
the one that came out the first time.

43
00:04:17,481 --> 00:04:23,101
Yes, right here you landed on the C,
just pay attention to it.

44
00:04:23,101 --> 00:04:26,166
This music is great for walking to work,
for being yourself.

45
00:04:26,166 --> 00:04:28,721
– On your own?
– Running, thinking, walking somewhere, driving,

46
00:04:28,721 --> 00:04:32,297
sitting in the evening with your beloved dog,
having a glass of your favourite wine.

47
00:04:32,297 --> 00:04:34,851
– Maybe a cup of coffee.
– Yes, or rather a nice cup of tea.

48
00:04:34,851 --> 00:04:38,938
– I've got a nine-bar phrase at the end.
– Then we'll record it separately, right?

49
00:04:38,938 --> 00:04:42,003
– Yes, in different takes.
– Mm-hm.

50
00:04:45,069 --> 00:04:48,645
Honestly, don't worry about the tuning.
I mean, when... when...

51
00:04:48,645 --> 00:04:52,732
No, I mean that in some pieces
you're really afraid to go for it...

52
00:04:52,732 --> 00:04:57,330
Yes, sometimes it actually sounds cool
when it sort of evens out.

53
00:05:04,482 --> 00:05:11,123
Only where there's a solo marked,
for example for Matvey, can you sing it out.

54
00:05:11,123 --> 00:05:15,721
Matvey, yes, do you have a solo?

55
00:05:17,765 --> 00:05:22,363
And it's honest, sincere material
that wasn't made in a rush or made for money.

56
00:05:22,363 --> 00:05:26,450
It's pure creativity.
It's a story about how, without trying

57
00:05:26,450 --> 00:05:31,047
to find easy money in it
or any kind of fame,

58
00:05:31,047 --> 00:05:35,645
we suddenly realised this material
genuinely interested us, from the heart.

59
00:05:35,645 --> 00:05:39,221
Something bigger, our beloved...

60
00:05:50,461 --> 00:05:55,059
I'd add one more thing to what Vanya said:
the music

61
00:05:55,059 --> 00:05:58,635
we made on this album
requires a certain

62
00:05:58,635 --> 00:06:02,722
inner maturity. And probably
until that moment we weren't ready

63
00:06:02,722 --> 00:06:07,320
to write these tracks, this music,
this genre.

64
00:06:07,320 --> 00:06:11,917
And probably the whole time, the reason we didn't do it earlier...
because we could have done it earlier,

65
00:06:11,917 --> 00:06:14,983
but we weren't ready.

66
00:06:31,331 --> 00:06:34,907
– You come in yourself, go on, go on.
– Well, so that in the studio, the part with me,

67
00:06:34,907 --> 00:06:38,483
she'd play it to the end.
– Well.

68
00:06:38,483 --> 00:06:43,081
– It's just that I had a lot of rests.
– Let's try it, yeah.

69
00:06:54,831 --> 00:06:58,407
Since we gave the musicians a lot of freedom
while playing: Matvey,

70
00:06:58,407 --> 00:07:01,983
Dasha...
– Who else? Dasha... Dasha was an absolute goddess.

71
00:07:01,983 --> 00:07:05,049
– Matvey and I have been together for many years,
I know him as a very experienced

72
00:07:05,049 --> 00:07:08,625
cellist.
– A wonderful one.

73
00:07:08,625 --> 00:07:12,201
– I know his strengths and weaknesses.
I know how to make sure

74
00:07:12,201 --> 00:07:15,266
he hears what I want.

75
00:07:19,353 --> 00:07:24,462
Dasha is a wonderful artist,
she helped us in so many ways.

76
00:07:24,462 --> 00:07:28,549
A real intellectual.
She takes her beautifully sounding viola

77
00:07:28,549 --> 00:07:33,147
and starts bringing in something all her own.
For many of the musicians we wrote specifically,

78
00:07:33,147 --> 00:07:37,745
meaning I knew almost exactly
that this person would be heard here,

79
00:07:37,745 --> 00:07:41,832
would sound exactly like this.
I knew what kind of sound they could make.

80
00:07:41,832 --> 00:07:46,429
I knew what Dasha could show,
what Matvey could do. But actually

81
00:07:46,429 --> 00:07:51,027
in many moments they outdid
themselves, because they were searching for their ideal selves.

82
00:07:51,027 --> 00:07:55,114
And truly, there's no limit to perfection,
and they found

83
00:07:55,114 --> 00:07:59,201
completely new sensations, a new sound.

84
00:08:11,462 --> 00:08:15,549
...and a lot of... This one, yes, right...

85
00:08:15,549 --> 00:08:19,636
Now I'll try to play it as if
it were two instruments.

86
00:08:19,636 --> 00:08:23,212
I'm trying to combine it somehow,
but what comes out is...

87
00:08:23,212 --> 00:08:27,299
It's very interesting to watch the musicians
getting used to freedom.

88
00:08:27,299 --> 00:08:30,876
Remember that moment when we say:
"Guys, we're starting the recording",

89
00:08:30,876 --> 00:08:35,984
specifically to Dasha or the girls...
Anya's... Anya's violin.

90
00:08:35,984 --> 00:08:39,561
We say: "Play whatever you want."
And at first she just... The first time

91
00:08:39,561 --> 00:08:43,648
it happened... The thing is, these musicians
are professionals,

92
00:08:43,648 --> 00:08:47,735
they're used to playing parts in an orchestra,
right. There are notes, there are set dynamics,

93
00:08:47,735 --> 00:08:51,311
there are the conductor's demands,
and here you're just told: "Do whatever you want."

94
00:08:51,311 --> 00:08:55,398
And for many, at first it's a kind of stupor,
because the person thinks: "How can I

95
00:08:55,398 --> 00:08:58,974
do anything at all, whatever I want?"
That's when the brain switches on

96
00:08:58,974 --> 00:09:03,572
and starts working in a completely different way,
and it was great to watch her,

97
00:09:03,572 --> 00:09:07,659
on the third or fourth take, realise
that she was so completely free

98
00:09:07,659 --> 00:09:10,724
that she could do anything she wanted.

99
00:09:10,724 --> 00:09:15,322
The musicians revealed new sides of themselves
during this work.

100
00:09:15,322 --> 00:09:19,409
– Well, yes, because there are noises in there,
things that in traditional classical music...

101
00:09:19,409 --> 00:09:22,985
well, just aren't used.
– What's more, they're actually removed,

102
00:09:22,985 --> 00:09:27,072
polished away.
– Yes. What do you want to point out?

103
00:09:27,072 --> 00:09:31,159
This... This was supposed to come from your prayer.

104
00:09:31,159 --> 00:09:36,268
You've infected him with your faith,
and the two of you start singing together.

105
00:09:36,268 --> 00:09:42,398
E-D-C-B-C, E-D-C-B-C, E-D-C-B-C.

106
00:09:42,398 --> 00:09:46,485
Three, four, and...

107
00:09:53,637 --> 00:09:58,235
By the way, you know, I noticed one thing:
the two of us even relate differently

108
00:09:58,235 --> 00:10:01,811
to the piano. Notice how we played.
Because, well, honestly,

109
00:10:01,811 --> 00:10:05,388
I always... When we started working,
I'd tell you: "Vanya, do something,

110
00:10:05,388 --> 00:10:09,475
we need a palette", and you'd just start travelling
through these harmonies.

111
00:10:09,475 --> 00:10:13,562
There's a small keyboard, I don't know
how many keys, but it's a mini one.

112
00:10:13,562 --> 00:10:17,649
I played the melody on top... to listen,
in unison, like an ensemble.

113
00:10:17,649 --> 00:10:20,714
It just looks really beautiful.
I sit down at the digital piano,

114
00:10:20,714 --> 00:10:25,312
how many keys? 88. Well, yes, a classic
full-size grand.

115
00:10:25,312 --> 00:10:28,888
Yes, and I start playing, and at the same time
we're set up in the same room

116
00:10:28,888 --> 00:10:32,464
literally at arm's length,
literally right now.

117
00:10:32,464 --> 00:10:36,040
And Zhenya is recording, capturing the track.
Zhenya, on top of me, sensing

118
00:10:36,040 --> 00:10:39,616
what I'm about to offer him, starts
playing this or that note,

119
00:10:39,616 --> 00:10:42,682
carefully adding to it.
– Suggesting, suggesting more.

120
00:10:42,682 --> 00:10:45,747
– I look at you like: what do you say?
And you're like: "Oh, nice."

121
00:10:45,747 --> 00:10:49,323
If, like... And yes, what we don't
catch with our eyes,

122
00:10:49,323 --> 00:10:52,388
we say out loud:
"Yes, great, keep it. Keep going."

123
00:10:52,388 --> 00:10:56,475
And that became the basis of those
sketches that we later developed.

124
00:10:56,475 --> 00:11:02,095
It's really cool that we have this live thing, yes...
so we could play some ideas

125
00:11:02,095 --> 00:11:06,693
on top of ourselves. Plus, after all, we do
have different views on this or that

126
00:11:06,693 --> 00:11:11,291
harmony and melody. That's probably the
coolest thing you can say.

127
00:11:22,530 --> 00:11:27,639
– Were you recording or not?
– We were. Okay. Any wishes?

128
00:11:27,639 --> 00:11:32,236
My wish is that you stay just as cool.

129
00:11:35,813 --> 00:11:40,410
Throughout almost the entire album
we were searching for certain colours.

130
00:11:40,410 --> 00:11:43,987
And those colours can't be captured
in notation, it isn't always convenient

131
00:11:43,987 --> 00:11:48,074
to write them down. Professional
21st-century composers run into this,

132
00:11:48,074 --> 00:11:52,672
they compose and invent loads and loads of techniques.
Getting to grips with all that takes

133
00:11:52,672 --> 00:11:57,269
a huge amount of time and effort.
So we searched quickly for the new

134
00:11:57,269 --> 00:12:01,356
effects we needed,
without going deep into the,

135
00:12:01,356 --> 00:12:04,933
how to put it, terminology, yes.
– Yes, and it should be said that this wasn't

136
00:12:04,933 --> 00:12:09,530
some textbook, typical orchestra recording,
where the orchestra plays a take

137
00:12:09,530 --> 00:12:13,617
from start to finish,
and that take is kept.

138
00:12:13,617 --> 00:12:18,215
Our approach was to record
a full take using our score,

139
00:12:18,215 --> 00:12:21,791
which Vanya wrote. After that we did
overdubs, where the musicians added

140
00:12:21,791 --> 00:12:27,411
nuances, certain moments,
through experimentation.

141
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:52,955
Perfect.

142
00:12:57,042 --> 00:13:00,618
The main thing, to start with, is to listen
to the album on headphones,

143
00:13:00,618 --> 00:13:03,683
not through a phone speaker.
There's a huge amount of detail in it.

144
00:13:03,683 --> 00:13:07,770
There's a lot of work on the space, yes.
It really is... Since the work was

145
00:13:07,770 --> 00:13:11,857
that of both a sound designer and a composer,
a composer and a sound designer

146
00:13:11,857 --> 00:13:14,922
for string instruments...
It's a kind of sound performance.

147
00:13:14,922 --> 00:13:19,009
There are so many details
you just can't take them all in.

148
00:13:19,009 --> 00:13:22,075
It's not just notes, there's something more.
– Yes, there are lots of sounds and effects,

149
00:13:22,075 --> 00:13:25,651
meaning besides the notes you also pick up
the texture itself.

150
00:13:25,651 --> 00:13:29,738
Did you know there's practically
egg frying in there?

151
00:13:29,738 --> 00:13:33,314
– I really do fry eggs in one track.
– You recorded that!

152
00:13:33,314 --> 00:13:35,868
– And I recorded it on a voice recorder and...
– How did you run it through the filters?

153
00:13:35,868 --> 00:13:39,445
– I just put a big, wide reverb on it,
and that's it. It came alive.

154
00:13:39,445 --> 00:13:43,532
– Reverberation... Is that something from the eggs too?
– It's a sound reflection effect, not eggs.

155
00:13:43,532 --> 00:13:47,619
But when Zhenya works with it,
fried eggs turn into a masterpiece.

156
00:13:47,619 --> 00:13:52,216
And it wasn't quite... not even droplets,
no, more like a running stream.

157
00:13:52,216 --> 00:13:56,303
It's really cool, totally different,
it doesn't smell of eggs at all. If you didn't say

158
00:13:56,303 --> 00:13:59,369
it's fried eggs, you might not realise.
– There was one track that really

159
00:13:59,369 --> 00:14:04,477
hit me personally,
if I can put it that way. You created a track

160
00:14:04,477 --> 00:14:09,586
called "Arma". It's actually
the story that touched me

161
00:14:09,586 --> 00:14:14,184
to the depths of my soul. You sketched it out...
It was about a minute and a half,

162
00:14:14,184 --> 00:14:18,271
a few themes. And I heard some bad news
about my hometown

163
00:14:18,271 --> 00:14:22,869
in the Ivanovo region, the town of Shuya.
It was damaged in a drone attack.

164
00:14:22,869 --> 00:14:26,445
And honestly, I was very frightened,
because my relatives were in that town.

165
00:14:26,445 --> 00:14:30,532
– And "Arma" actually translates
as "weapons".

166
00:14:30,532 --> 00:14:34,619
– Yes, and that track really carried
a reflection of reality,

167
00:14:34,619 --> 00:14:38,195
that people are doing cruel things right now,
stupid things...

168
00:14:38,195 --> 00:14:41,260
– Mass aggression.
– Somehow insane.

169
00:14:41,260 --> 00:14:44,837
And somehow the limits got lost, when will this all end,
how do we end it all.

170
00:14:44,837 --> 00:14:48,413
People just keep committing this cruelty.
And you send me this track.

171
00:14:48,413 --> 00:14:52,500
And it resonated with me as a reflection
of what I was feeling at that moment:

172
00:14:52,500 --> 00:14:56,076
I was worried about people's fates.
– And by the way, after that we wrote

173
00:14:56,076 --> 00:14:59,652
the very last track...
– The last track, which then...

174
00:14:59,652 --> 00:15:03,228
which we later called "Untitled".
– A sort of lullaby.

175
00:15:03,228 --> 00:15:07,315
– A lullaby, where you really need
to come to your senses, calm down.

176
00:15:07,315 --> 00:15:11,913
– That's great. But yes, by the way, this pairing
is probably the most emotional on the album,

177
00:15:11,913 --> 00:15:16,000
it's directly connected with our
own reflection.
